findings.body:
The new `write_and_make_executable` returns `Result<(), FileWriteError>`, but `prepare_tools` returns `crate::Result<PathBuf>`. The `?` operator needs `From<FileWriteError> for crate::Error`. No such impl exists. `crate::Error` only has `#[from]` derives for specific types (`io::Error`, `anyhow::Error`, and others), and nothing generic covers an arbitrary `std::error::Error`. The build fails at every call site in `prepare_tools`: lines 232, 239, 245, 251, and 259.

The codebase already has a pattern for this. `error.rs` implements `ErrorExt::fs_context` for `Result<T, io::Error>`, which wraps the failure as `Error::Fs { context, path, error }`. That gives the same context without a new error type.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="309-316"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The custom enum and its Display/Error impls add about 25 lines to avoid a conversion the existing `Error::Fs` variant already provides.

`write_and_make_executable` now calls `fs::set_permissions` before `fs::write`. On a fresh tools directory, the target file does not exist yet. `set_permissions` on a missing path returns `ENOENT`. Every first-time write therefore fails at this line: AppRun, linuxdeploy, the GStreamer script, and the optional appimage plugin.

The base code wrote the file first and then set its mode. That order is correct and should be restored.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="309-316"}

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="312-313"}
fs::write(path, data)?;
fs::set_permissions(path, fs::Permissions::from_mode(0o770))?;

The base code wrote the GTK plugin only when it was missing: `if !gtk.exists()`. This PR changes that to `if gtk.exists()`. On a fresh tools directory, the file does not exist, so the block is skipped and `linuxdeploy-plugin-gtk.sh` is never created. Linuxdeploy then fails to find the GTK plugin during bundling.

The GStreamer block right below still uses `!gstreamer.exists()`, which makes the inversion look like a mistake. The GStreamer block also has the chmod-before-write problem described above, so both plugins are broken on a clean machine.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="243-251"}

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="243"}
  if !gtk.exists() {

The base code computed `linuxdeploy_arch` (which maps `i686` to `i383`) and used it in both the local filename and the download URL. This PR changes the URL to use the raw `arch` value. The file on disk is still `linuxdeploy-i383.AppImage`, but it is now downloaded from `linuxdeploy-i686.AppImage`.

The PR description says nothing about this. It looks like an accidental edit while touching this line. If the release asset is named `i383`, i686 users will get a 404 at bundle time. If the author intended the change, the PR should say why.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="236-240"}

This PR adds no tests. The bugs above would have been caught by a small test. For example, calling `write_and_make_executable` on a path in a temp directory and checking the file contents and mode would have flagged the chmod-before-write ordering. Checking that `prepare_tools` writes the GTK script into an empty directory would have caught the inverted condition.

The bundler crate already has Linux-only paths. The helper and the `prepare_tools` existence checks can be tested with `tempfile` and no network access. Because these functions do real network downloads, the test should target the helper and the embedded-file branches, not the full download flow.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="309-316"}

`linuxdeploy-plugin-gtk.sh` and `linuxdeploy-plugin-gstreamer.sh` are now copies of upstream scripts from the linuxdeploy plugin repos. Their headers do not say where they came from, which upstream commit they match, or their license. Once they are vendored, they will drift from upstream and nobody will know which version was copied.

Add a short header comment to each script with the source URL, the upstream commit SHA or tag, and the license. Also note in the `.changes` file or a README that these files are maintained in-tree.

